\begin_layout Standard
The modern landscape of computational biology is characterized by two main ecosystems:
 the statistical methods available through Bioconductor,
 rooted in the R programming language,
 and the rapid growth of machine learning frameworks within the Python ecosystem.
 Bioconductor has served as the gold standard for high-throughput genomic analysis by providing standardized data structures and reproducible statistical methods,
 where as Python has quickly become the primary environment for deep learning,
 image analysis,
 and general-purpose data science.
 This presents a challenge to the scientific community,
 navigating complex,
 fragile,
 and often inefficient interoperability solutions to leverage the strengths of both worlds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We present BiocPy,
 a software framework and ecosystem designed to bridge this gap.
 BiocPy is not a wrapper for R functions rather,
 it re-engineers the foundational data structures of Bioconductor,
 specifically GenomicRanges,
 SummarizedExperiment,
 SingleCellExperiment etc.
 as native Python data representations.
 Furthermore,
 BiocPy provides the rds2py package facilitating the direct ingestion of serialized R data objects (RDS files) into Python environments.
 Using BiocPy's data structures enables seamless,
 polyglot analysis workflows for e.g.
 the scran/singleR collection of tools for single-cell RNA-seq analysis.
 All packages in the BiocPy ecosystem are published to Python's Package Index (PyPI) 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
target "http://github.com/BiocPy"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Bioinformatics is an interdisciplinary field at the intersection of molecular biology,
 statistics,
 and computer science.
 Historically,
 these disciplines have favored different computational tools,
 leading to a diverse software landscape to perform genomic analyses.
 Bioconductor
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "gentleman_bioconductor_2004"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 built upon the R programming language,
 it was designed with a primary focus on statistical analysis and visualization of genomic data.
 Over the last two decades,
 it has grown into a repository of over 2,000 packages,
 conforming to a strict set of shared data structures and design principles.
 The strength of Bioconductor lies in its community-driven standardization and peer-review process,
 objects such as GenomicRanges
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "lawrence_software_2013"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and SummarizedExperiment
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "noauthor_summarizedexperiment_nodate"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 provide a common currency for data exchange,
 allowing developers to quickly build and iterate methods without reinventing new data structures.
 For researchers focused on rigorous statistical testing methods and established genomic workflows,
 Bioconductor remains the standard environment to perform these analyses.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Python has emerged over the last decade as the dominant language of data science and machine learning (ML).
 Powered by libraries such as NumPy
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "harris_array_2020"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 PyTorch
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "paszke_pytorch_2019"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 and TensorFlow
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "abadi_tensorflow_2016"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 it provides flexibility,
 and integration with modern computing paradigms (distributed computing,
 ease of GPU access etc).
 In the context of genomics,
 Python has seen a surge in popularity driven by the rise of single-cell analysis through the scverse efforts
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "virshup_scverse_2023"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 where large-scale analyses benefit significantly from ML libraries (e.g.
 rapids-singlecell
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "dicks_scverserapids_singlecell_2024"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The coexistence of these two powerful computational ecosystems creates a fragmentation problem.
 A typical modern genomic workflow might require the robust methods available in Bioconductor (R),
 followed by the application of a deep learning models or inference methods available in Python.
 Traditionally,
 researchers navigated this by:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
File-based exchange:
 
\series default
Saving intermediate results in CSV or text files.
 This is error-prone,
 loses metadata,
 and is computationally expensive due to repeated serialization and deserialization when dealing with a large cohort of samples.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Interface libraries:
 
\series default
Using tools like rpy2
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "noauthor_rpy2rpy2_2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 to run R code within Python.
 While functional,
 rpy2 requires the user to maintain two complex runtime environments (R and Python) simultaneously,
 leading to dependency hell,
 installation fragility,
 and significant overhead in context switching.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Reimplementation:
 
\series default
Rewriting statistical methods in Python.
 This often leads to subtle differences in results,
 lacks the peer-reviewed rigor of the original Bioconductor implementation,
 and contributes to incompatibility in standard data models (e.g.,
 the divergence between Bioconductor's SingleCellExperiment
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "amezquita_orchestrating_2020"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and scverse's AnnData
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "virshup_anndata_2024"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocPy addresses this gap,
 rather than forcing R to run inside Python (vice-versa),
 or creating an entirely new,
 incompatible Python-native standard,
 BiocPy ports Bioconductor’s core class definitions into Python.
 A SummarizedExperiment in BiocPy is designed to have the same slots,
 and the same behavioral guarantees as its R counterpart.
 This ensures that the conceptual data model remains consistent,
 reducing the cognitive burden for researchers transitioning between languages.
 By adhering to Bioconductor’s design principles,
 such as the separation of assay data from row/column metadata and the use of functional programming patterns,
 BiocPy enables a workflow where data can round-trip between R and Python.
 Furthermore,
 By providing Python bindings to these same C++ libraries (e.g.,
 libscran
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "noauthor_libscran_nodate"
literal "false"

\end_inset

),
 BiocPy allows Python users to access the exact same methods in R,
 ensuring consistent interpretation and reliability in results.
 These efforts create a bridge between computational platforms and enables researches to focus their time on biological questions rather than technical challenges.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocPy is not the only attempt to solve the R/Python divide,
 but its approach is unique.
 Unlike rpy2,
 which is powerful but heavy due to embedding an R instance,
 it's often difficult to configure in containerized environments (Docker/Singularity).
 BiocPy is lightweight and native.
 AnnData is excellent for single-cell matrices but lacks the generality of SummarizedExperiment (which handles any matrix data) and does not support GenomicRanges (or range based operations).
 BiocPy supports a broader spectrum of genomic data types.
 Finally,
 while writing genomic tools in pure Python is possible,
 it often leads to poor performance or lack of standardization;
 BiocPy provides the necessary structure for stable and sustainable software development.
\end_layout

