\begin_layout Section
Core Data Structures
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The development of BiocPy is not just a translation of R code into Python,
 it is adoption of Bioconductor’s design philosophy into a Pythonic context.
 The development of the ecosystem is guided by adherence to specific design principles that prioritize reliability,
 interoperability and usability.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Functional Programming and Immutability:
 
\series default
An important feature of the BiocPy ecosystem is its reliance on functional programming paradigms.
 In the broader Python data science world,
 libraries like pandas
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "mckinney_pandas_nodate"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 often encourage in-place modification of objects (e.g.,
 df.drop(...,
 inplace=True)).
 While convenient for simple scripts,
 in-place mutation can be dangerous in complex genomic pipelines where multiple analysis steps reference the same underlying data object.
 Side effects from one function can inadvertently corrupt the state of the data for downstream processes.
 BiocPy mitigates this by adopting a functional approach similar to R.
 Operations that modify an object typically return a new instance rather than altering the original.
 This approach enhances the safety of modular workflows,
 ensuring that the input data remains invariant unless explicitly overwritten.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Python-Native Implementation with Minimal Dependencies:
 
\series default
While the conceptual architecture borrows from R,
 the implementation is strictly Python-native.
 BiocPy leverages the well established scientific Python stack,
 NumPy for numerical arrays,
 SciPy
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "virtanen_scipy_2020"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for sparse matrices,
 to ensure high performance and compatibility.
 The ecosystem maintains a lean dependency footprint.
 By avoiding unnecessary heavy libraries,
 BiocPy ensures that its core packages (BiocFrame,
 GenomicRanges,
 SummarizedExperiment) can be installed easily in diverse environments.
 This lightweight design facilitates the creation of clean environments using Conda or pip,
 minimizing the dependency conflicts that plague complex bioinformatics software stacks.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
BiocFrame:
 A Flexible DataFrame
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The pandas DataFrame is ubiquitous in most analysis workflows.
 However,
 for genomic data,
 pandas presents several limitations that BiocFrame addresses.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Type Preservation:
 
\series default
One of the most persistent issues with pandas in a bioinformatics context is its aggressive type inference and coercion.
 For example,
 introducing a missing value (NaN) into an integer column in pandas typically forces the entire column to be cast to floating-point,
 as integer type does not support NaN.
 In genomics,
 the distinction between an integer count of 0,
 a count of 1,
 and a missing value is critical.
 BiocFrame follows the Bioconductor principle of "what you put is what you get".
 It preserves the original data types of columns,
 ensuring that integer counts remain integers and that complex objects are stored as intended.
 This type stability is crucial when the data represents biological measurements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Nested and Complex Data:
 
\series default
Genomic data is rarely flat.
 A single row representing a gene might have multiple associated pieces of metadata that are themselves lists or complex objects (e.g.,
 a list of exons,
 a set of protein domains,
 or a GenomicRanges object representing its locus).
 pandas generally struggles with columns containing complex objects,
 often relegating them to a slow,
 generic object dtype.
 BiocFrame is designed to support nested data structures natively.
 It can hold columns that are themselves BiocFrame objects or lists of varying lengths.
 This allows for the construction of recursive,
 hierarchical models that accurately reflect the biological data,
 rather than flattening everything into a 2D table.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Functional Programming Paradigm:
 
\series default
As mentioned in the design philosophy,
 BiocPy,
 mirroring Bioconductor,
 adopts a functional programming paradigm.
 BiocFrame operations typically return new objects.
 This immutability-by-default approach,
 while different from standard Pythonic patterns,
 is safer for complex scientific pipelines where tracking the state of data is important.
 This applies not only to BiocFrame but all packages within BiocPy.
\end_layout

