\begin_layout Subsection
GenomicRanges
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The most crucial feature of Bioconductor is its handling of genomic intervals via the GenomicRanges package.
 In Python,
 interval arithmetic has historically been handled by wrapping command-line tools (like bedtools
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "quinlan_bedtools_2010"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) or using standalone libraries like pyranges.
 BiocPy brings a native,
 in-memory implementation of GenomicRanges to Python.
 The BiocPy implementation decomposes genomic information into three interacting classes:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Sequence Information:
 
\series default
Stores the "universe" of the experiment,
 the chromosome names and their lengths.
 This prevents invalid operations,
 such as defining a range that extends beyond the end of a chromosome.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Ranges:

\series default
 Implements generic integer interval arithmetic.
 This calculates overlaps,
 gaps,
 and shifts,
 independent of biological context.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Genomic metadata:
 
\series default
Adds the biological layer,
 chromosome names (sequence names) and strand information (+,
 -,
 or *) to the IRanges.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocPy’s GenomicRanges abstracts the coordinate system,
 providing methods to import from commonly used annotation file formats or R objects while maintaining internal consistency.
 The availability of methods like findOverlaps,
 nearest,
 and resize in native Python allows for complex analyses.
 For example,
 one can load a set of ChIP-seq peaks and a set of gene annotations,
 and in a few lines of Python,
 determine which genes have peaks in their promoter regions,
 handling strand-specificity automatically.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Containers for Experimental Data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To encapsulate the complexity of high-throughput experiments,
 BiocPy provides container classes that bind assay data to metadata.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
SummarizedExperiment:
 
\series default
The SummarizedExperiment (SE) class is the fundamental container for matrix-like data.
 It enforces a strict geometry:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Assays:
 
\series default
A list of matrices (e.g.,
 counts,
 log-counts) where rows are features and columns are samples.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
RowData:
 
\series default
A BiocFrame (or GenomicRanges in the case of RangedSummarizedExperiment [rowRanges]) describing the rows.
 Must match the number of rows in assays.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
ColData:
 
\series default
A BiocFrame describing the columns (samples).
 Must match the number of columns in assays.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
SingleCellExperiment:
 
\series default
Extending SE,
 the SingleCellExperiment (SCE) is tailored for scRNA-seq.
 It adds slots for:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
ReducedDims:
 
\series default
Storing embeddings like PCA,
 t-SNE,
 and UMAP coordinates directly within the object.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
AltExps:
 
\series default
Storing "Alternative Experiments." In modern single-cell workflows,
 a cell might have RNA data,
 but also Spike-in controls,
 or Antibody Derived Tags (CITE-seq).
 AltExps allows these auxiliary matrices to be stored within the main object,
 linked to the same cells but with different feature sets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This design contrasts with the AnnData object used in scverse (scanpy) ecosystem.
 While AnnData is powerful,
 SingleCellExperiment offers a stricter adherence to the Bioconductor S4 model,
 support genomic range operations and facilitating interoperability with R tools.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
MultiAssayExperiment:
 
\series default
For complex study designs involving multiple assays performed on the same subjects (e.g.,
 a cohort with RNA-seq,
 mutation calls,
 and copy number variants),
 BiocPy implements MultiAssayExperiment
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "noauthor_software_nodate"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This container manages a collection of experiments (which can be SummarizedExperiment objects or its derivatives) and a sample map that links biological specimens to the specific assays performed on them.
 This structure is vital for multi-omics integration,
 allowing for coordinated subsetting across diverse data modalities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And many more packages for efficiently handling and lazily operating on data on-disk (delayed arrays),
 and extending summarized experiment representations to support spatial modalities as shown in Table 1.
 
\end_layout

