\begin_layout Subsection
Integration with Broader Python Ecosystem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocPy acts as a central hub,
 not just connecting to R but also to the broader Python ecosystem.
 We take advantage of existing efforts in Python whenever possible
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
scverse:
 
\series default
The AnnData format is the standard for the scverse ecosystem (scanpy).
 BiocPy provides utilities to convert SingleCellExperiment objects to AnnData objects.
 This allows users to mix and match tools:
 utilizing Bioconductor’s statistical methods for normalization and feature selection,
 then converting to AnnData to use Scanpy’s visualization tools or scvi-tools for probabilistic modeling.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Pandas or Polars:
 
\series default
As noted in the BiocFrame section,
 the integration with pandas ensures that BiocPy objects can easily interface with general-purpose data cleaning and visualization libraries.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Enabling Methods Development and Polyglot Workflows
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The true value of BiocPy lies not just in data representations and storage,
 but in enabling robust methods development and flexible,
 iterative analysis pipelines.
 By providing standard data structures and shared C++ backends,
 BiocPy allows developers to build tools that are reproducible across languages and allows analysts to leverage the best of both worlds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Shared C++ libraries
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Multi-modal Single-Cell Analysis using scranpy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Reproducibility is often compromised when methods are re-implemented in different languages due to subtle differences in algorithms.
 BiocPy addresses this by binding to the same standalone C++ libraries used by R packages.
 For example,
 the scran
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "lun_step-by-step_2016"
literal "false"

\end_inset

/scrapper
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "noauthor_scrapper_nodate,lun_powering_2023"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 package is a staple of scRNA-seq analysis in R
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "amezquita_orchestrating_2020"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Its core logic resides in libscran,
 a C++ library.
 BiocPy’s scranpy links to this same library.
 When a user performs log-normalization or feature selection in Python using scranpy,
 they are executing the exact same compiled code as an R user.
 This guarantees that the statistical transformations applied to the data are identical,
 preserving the validity of downstream comparisons.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset listings
